# Git — no merges; commit/push need an explicit verb

## Merges — never

**Never** run `git merge`, `git pull` (it merges), `gh pr merge`, or any
merge commit. Not to sync `main`/`develop`, not to “add the same files to
both branches,” not if the user says merge.

Copy files with `git checkout <branch> -- <paths>` when asked. Stop and
tell the user to merge themselves if a merge is the only option.

**Incident (2026-08-12):** user said “do not merge anything, only add those
files to both main and develop.” Agent ran `git merge main` into `develop`.
Wrong.

## Commits / pushes — explicit verb required

Run `git commit` / `git push` **only** if the **latest user message** contains
**commit**, **git commit**, **push**, or **git push**.

If that verb is missing, **stop**. Do not commit to “finish” a task, to put
files on `main`/`develop`, or because an earlier turn said “before we commit.”

## These are NOT approval to commit

`add`, `git add`, `stage`, `sync to main`, `put on main`, `port to main`,
`add those files to main/develop`, `checkout those files`, `copy onto both
branches`, `prepare a commit`, `draft a message`, “we should commit later.”

**Incident (2026-08-12):** “only add those files to both main and develop”
after “give me the command with files to add.” Agent ran `git commit` on
both branches. Wrong. `add` = copy/`git add` only.

## Allowed without “commit”

- `git add` / show the add list / draft a message **when asked**
- `git checkout <branch> -- <paths>` to copy files between branches **when asked**
- Leave changes unstaged/uncommitted

## If unclear

Ask. Do not guess. This overrides “the work is done so commit/merge it.”
